"Connects locations of equal value to describe a continuous geographic field such as temperature, pressure or elevation.\n\nUse when: Show spatial gradients and approximate threshold locations. Communicate a measured or modeled surface beyond administrative boundaries.\n\nAvoid when: The phenomenon consists of unrelated regional categories or observations cannot justify interpolation.\n\nVisual grammar: Labeled lines connect equal values under one unit convention. A declared interval determines contour spacing in value. Optional filled bands follow the same levels and retain missing-area boundaries.\n\nBuild: Choose a continuous variable and document the observation or model source. Create a defensible surface and select meaningful contour levels. Label lines clearly and disclose interpolation limits or uncertain coverage.\n\nCheck: Contour values and intervals match the source surface. Close line spacing indicates a steep gradient under the same interval. Keep labels clear and use layers for dense surface and base features.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing smooth contours from sparse data without acknowledging uncertainty. Treating interpolated values as direct measurements everywhere.\n\nExample: A regional temperature map connects equal modeled temperatures, showing cooler uplands and warmer valleys with stated units and observation time.\n\nAlternatives: Topographic map, Isochrone map."
